 "questions": [
  {
   "id": 464011,
   "is_answer_order_important": false,
   "stimulus_html": "",
   "stem_html": "Paul was trying to analyze the results of an experiment on the effects of music on plant growth, but he kept facing unexpected data. He was using automated instrumentation to collect growth data on the plants at periodic time intervals. He found that some of the data points were much higher or lower than anticipated, and he suspected that there was noise (error) present in the data. He wanted to investigate the main source of the error in the data so that he could take corrective actions and thus minimize the impact of the error(s) on the experiment results. What is the most likely source of error that Paul might encounter in this experiment: human error, instrumentation error, or sampling error? Consider the various sources of noise (error).",
